  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={3}^{x/2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={3}^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit up and obtain the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b+1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={3}^{x}$, we have that ${3}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{(2a)/2}+1 \\\\  & ={3}^{a}+1 \\\\  & =b+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b+1)$ must lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$",
